# stiq relay — Tor v3 hidden service config (PLAN.md §3.1, Step 3)
#
# Fronts the local relay (127.0.0.1:3334) as a v3 .onion. The relay binds loopback only;
# Tor forwards onion traffic to it locally. There is NO clearnet exposure.
#
# ── This file is a MERGED REFERENCE, not what's live ──────────────────────────────────────────
# deploy/stiq-up.sh never installs this file verbatim. It writes the equivalent directives as
# separate drop-ins under /etc/tor/torrc.d/ (loaded via `%include /etc/tor/torrc.d/*.conf`
# appended to the box's real /etc/tor/torrc):
#   stiq-base.conf        — SocksPort 0 + HiddenServiceNonAnonymousMode/SingleHopMode 1
#                            (RELAY_SINGLE_ONION=1, the default; ABSENT under --no-single-onion)
#   stiq-controlport.conf — ControlPort unix:/var/run/tor/control + CookieAuthentication 1
#                            (ONLY when STIQ_VANGUARDS=1 AND RELAY_SINGLE_ONION=0)
#   stiq-relay.conf        — the relay HiddenService block below (+ optional SSH-recovery block)
#   stiq-dashboard.conf    — the dashboard HiddenService block (DASHBOARD_ONION=1, the default)
# This file shows them all merged together (as the DEFAULT, single-onion-on config actually
# behaves) so the whole picture reads in one place. Treat it as documentation/source-of-truth for
# the Tor hardening, not as a file you `cp` into place — see relay/deploy/README.md.

DataDirectory /var/lib/tor

# --- Zero logging (PLAN.md §3.2) -------------------------------------------------------
# Discard logs entirely. Do NOT add a 'Log notice ...' line: we never want access logs,
# connection logs, or IP addresses written anywhere. SafeLogging scrubs any sensitive
# strings should a log line ever be emitted.
Log err file /dev/null
SafeLogging 1

# --- Single-onion mode (RELAY_SINGLE_ONION=1, the DEFAULT — stiq-base.conf) -------------
# Tor's official "single onion service" mode. Every HiddenService this PROCESS fronts (relay +
# dashboard + the optional SSH-recovery onion below) drops its own 3-hop path to the rendezvous
# point down to a single hop — trimming a full circuit build off first-connect (a meaningful slice
# of onion connect latency/jitter). This affects ONLY the server's own circuit-anonymity toward the
# rendezvous point; it is invisible to clients, who still reach a completely normal v3 .onion the
# completely normal (3-hop, anonymous) way. Acceptable here because this box's location/operator
# was never actually hidden (rented hosting, SSH access, billing trail) — only members' connections
# are meant to stay anonymous, and this does not touch that.
#
# These are PROCESS-WIDE options, not per-HiddenService — they apply to every onion below.
# SocksPort 0 (further down) is REQUIRED the instant either of these is 1; tor refuses to start
# otherwise. Comment out both (and drop SocksPort back to its default) to run the standard,
# fully-anonymous-both-ways hidden service instead — equivalent to `stiq-up.sh --no-single-onion`.
HiddenServiceNonAnonymousMode 1
HiddenServiceSingleHopMode 1

# --- Hidden service v3 -----------------------------------------------------------------
HiddenServiceDir /var/lib/tor/stiq-relay/
HiddenServiceVersion 3
# onion:80  ->  local relay WebSocket. Clients connect to ws://<onion>/ (default port 80).
HiddenServicePort 80 127.0.0.1:3334

# Reliability: more introduction points => more independent rendezvous entry paths, so the
# service stays reachable if some intro points are slow, attacked, or churning (default 3,
# max 20). 5 is a good resilience/overhead balance for a single long-lived relay.
HiddenServiceNumIntroductionPoints 5

# Intro-point denial-of-service defenses for the hidden service. These raise the cost of
# flooding the service without identifying any client (defense in depth on top of the
# app-layer allow list).
HiddenServiceEnableIntroDoSDefense 1
HiddenServiceEnableIntroDoSRatePerSec 25
HiddenServiceEnableIntroDoSBurstPerSec 200

# Onion-service proof-of-work DoS defense (tor >= 0.4.8; this deploy targets >= 0.4.9).
# The PoW puzzle is OFF until the service is actually under load, then it prioritises clients
# that solve a small puzzle — throttling floods without any client-side UX (modern clients,
# incl. the bundled IPtProxy/Tor, solve it transparently). Pairs with the intro-DoS defense
# above: intro-DoS rate-limits introductions, PoW prioritises rendezvous under sustained load.
#
# REQUIRES a tor built WITH the 'pow' module (equix). The official Tor Project apt repo
# (deb.torproject.org) ships it; some third-party builds (e.g. Homebrew) do NOT, and tor will
# refuse to start with: "this build of tor does not include the required 'pow' module".
# Verify with:  tor --verify-config -f /etc/tor/torrc   (and `tor --list-modules` shows pow: yes)
# If your build lacks it, comment out the three HiddenServicePoW* lines below.
HiddenServicePoWDefensesEnabled 1
HiddenServicePoWQueueRate 250
HiddenServicePoWQueueBurst 2500

# Long-lived high-value service: vanguards-lite (pinned layer-2 guards) is built in and ON by
# default since tor 0.4.7 — no config needed. For layer-3 guards + Rendguard/Bandguards, run
# the standalone `vanguards` addon against the ControlPort below (leave HSLayer2/3Nodes UNSET;
# static pinning is a deanonymisation risk — let the addon manage them).
#
# ⚠ INCOMPATIBLE with single-onion mode above: vanguards defends the hidden service's own 3-hop
# path to its rendezvous point, and HiddenServiceSingleHopMode removes that path entirely — there
# is nothing left for vanguards to protect. deploy/stiq-up.sh only emits the ControlPort lines
# below (as a SEPARATE drop-in, stiq-controlport.conf) and installs the vanguards addon when
# STIQ_VANGUARDS=1 AND RELAY_SINGLE_ONION=0 (--no-single-onion) — see step 8/12b there. This is a
# reconciliation of a formerly-stale claim: earlier versions of this comment (and of
# stiq-vanguards.service's) said ControlPort was "always on"; it was NEVER emitted by any live
# torrc.d drop-in — only this unused reference file carried it — so vanguards silently had no
# control socket to attach to until stiq-up.sh started writing it conditionally.
ControlPort unix:/var/run/tor/control
CookieAuthentication 1
# See: https://github.com/mikeperry-tor/vanguards
#
# Zero-downtime restarts / HA: reload config with `kill -HUP` (after `tor --verify-config`)
# instead of a cold restart; for true HA put OnionBalance v3 in front of N backends sharing
# this onion key. See: https://blog.torproject.org/cooking-onions-reclaiming-onionbalance/

# Strongest gate: v3 client authorization — ON BY DEFAULT, provisioned by the installer.
# Only clients holding the shared community auth key can even resolve/reach the onion
# (defeats "a government actor who discovers the .onion address"). This needs NO torrc
# directive: tor enforces client-auth purely from the PRESENCE of authorized_clients/*.auth
# in the HiddenServiceDir. deploy/stiq-up.sh does it — it mints ONE shared community
# keypair, writes /var/lib/tor/stiq-relay/authorized_clients/community.auth, and emits the
# matching private key into the v4 community code every member holds (which the client loads
# via ClientOnionAuthDir). Run the installer with RELAY_ONION_AUTH=0 to leave the onion
# public instead (community.auth removed); the v4 code then carries an inert reach key.
# See: https://community.torproject.org/onion-services/advanced/client-auth/

# --- This is a service-only node, not a relay/exit/SOCKS proxy -------------------------
# SocksPort 0 serves double duty: it was already correct policy for a service-only node (no
# outbound SOCKS anonymity offered to anything on this box), and it is now ALSO a hard REQUIREMENT
# of single-onion mode above (tor refuses HiddenServiceNonAnonymousMode/SingleHopMode without it).
#
# Consequence: relay/main.go's own Safe-Browsing SOCKS use (STIQ_TOR_SOCKS, default
# 127.0.0.1:9050) can no longer use THIS tor instance. When Safe-Browsing is configured
# (safe_browsing_api_key set) AND single-onion is on, deploy/stiq-up.sh provisions a SEPARATE,
# minimal, client-only tor instance (Debian tor-instance-create convention: SocksPort only, no
# HiddenService blocks — see /etc/tor/instances/stiq-client/torrc) purely to keep that default
# resolving. See step 8c in deploy/stiq-up.sh. Not needed when Safe-Browsing is unconfigured (it
# fails closed / 503s by design either way) or when running with --no-single-onion.
SocksPort 0
ExitRelay 0
ExitPolicy reject *:*
